WHAT CHANGED IN THIS BUILD (all four have a reading; ledger 664-672):
  1. esc_sugate7: the boot chain opens 7's su-name gate.  The site is the one instruction that CONSUMES
     the gate's verdict (do_execveat_common+0x40 `tbnz w0,#0x1f` -> `mov w19,#-1` = EPERM), written to a
     NOP through a 1 GB alias placed in an empty pgd slot.  Memory-only; `adb reboot` restores the gate.
     SUPERSEDED NOTE (1.9.16): this build's own log shows the app path DID print `sugate7 alias PROVEN`
     (ledger 670), so "the app cannot read /proc/sys/kernel/modprobe" was never a reading -- it was my
     inference from the code's fallback branch.  The branch itself was the real defect: on a boot where
     that read fails, this build patches .text without any window witness.  1.9.16 moves the read to
     the caller (over the daemon's root channel) and refuses the patch when it does not move.
  2. relayd serves each caller in its own child (an interactive session no longer blocks other callers).
  3. the liveness probe runs off the main thread.  It used to throw NetworkOnMainThreadException on every
     call, which made `alive()` answer "unreachable" on healthy boots and sent EscService to spend a
     SECOND kernel needle in the same boot -- that, not the alias or the patched instruction, is what
     rebooted the phone twice (ledger 672).
  4. selfGrant no longer rewrites an allow list it cannot read, so root-owned lists keep the owner's
     third-party entries (measured: a root-owned 3-line list survived a full chain run).
Product semantics unchanged: recovery from a mid-boot daemon death is a reboot or a hand on the escalate
button, one needle per boot at most.  Bootloader never unlocked, no partition written.
